{
  "requirement": "Saved configuration MUST remain distinct from resolved runtime configuration.\nSerializing and deserializing a valid configuration MUST preserve its semantic\nvalues, including explicit versus omitted settings. Optional fields MAY be\nomitted on serialization; defaults and environment-derived values MUST NOT be\nmaterialized into omitted fields. TOML comments and formatting need not\nround-trip.\n\nLoading configuration or running a configured feature MUST NOT rewrite the user\nor project configuration as a side effect. Resolved credentials and live runtime\nobjects MUST NOT enter the serialized data structures. Explicitly supplied\ncredential values are permitted only in the fields documented by the authoring\nfeature; credentials resolved from a profile or environment MUST NOT be written\nback to configuration.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "## Judgment\n\nThe sources satisfy `requirements.configuration.serialization`. The implementation is complete and marked, and the tests cover the points of the design space I checked. I found no acknowledged gaps (no FIXME markers).\n\n## Implementation\n\n**Saved data is separate from resolved runtime data.**\n- The saved structures in `src/config.rs` (`UserConfig`, `WorkspaceConfig`, `UserAuthoringConfig`, `AuthoringConfig`, `BedrockConfig`, `OllamaConfig`, `BedrockCredentials`, `OptionValue`) hold only what the file states.\n- `ResolvedAuthoringConfig` is a different type and does not implement `Serialize`.\n- Environment-derived values come only from `OllamaConfig::api_key_from` (the `api_key` wrapper delegates to it). It returns a transient `String` and never writes back to the struct.\n- The Bedrock profile, region and credentials are handed straight to the AWS SDK loader in `providers.rs`. No live client or resolved credential is stored in a serializable structure.\n\n**Omitted settings stay omitted.**\n- Every optional field uses `#[serde(default, skip_serializing_if = ...)]`, for example `endpoint`, `concurrency`, `aws_profile`, `region`, `api_key_env`, `credentials`, `options`, `projects`, and the workspace `configs`.\n- Defaults such as the Ollama endpoint (`endpoint()`) and the effective concurrency (`effective_concurrency()`) are computed on read. They are never written into the fields.\n- `UserAuthoringConfig.configs` has no skip attribute, so an explicit empty `configs = {}` survives a round trip.\n- Empty `projects`, workspace `configs` and `evidence.allow` or `disallow` collapse to omitted. That is semantically identical, and the requirement allows omitting optional fields.\n\n**Round trip.**\n- `to_toml` validates the config and then calls `toml::to_string_pretty`.\n- `parse` and the load functions use `decode` followed by `validate`.\n- Types derive `PartialEq`, so the tests can check semantic equality after a round trip.\n- `OptionValue` rejects non-finite floats, so serialization stays lossless.\n\n**No rewriting as a side effect.**\n- `load_user_config` and `load_workspace_config` only read files.\n- A search of `src` for file writes found none that touch configuration. The only writes target evidence files, usage logs, and `setup`'s `book.toml`.\n- The resolve functions take shared references only.\n\n**Credentials.**\n- Explicit credentials exist only in the documented fields (`credentials.*`, `api_key`). `SecretString` serializes transparently, so explicit values round-trip, and its Debug output is redacted.\n- Resolved or env-derived secrets have no field to land in.\n\n**Marking.**\n- All the code that carries out the obligations carries `{impl}`: `parse`, `to_toml`, `load_*`, `api_key[_from]`, and the resolve functions.\n- Two readers, `endpoint()` and `effective_concurrency()`, keep defaults out of the saved fields. They are marked for the authoring requirements rather than this one. They only read, so I did not treat that as a gap.\n- The `impl` code contains no behavior outside this requirement and the other definitions it carries.\n\n## Tests (`tests/configuration.rs`)\n\n- `absent_and_minimal_configuration_are_distinct_and_read_only`: an absent file gives `None` and no file is created. A minimal file is left byte-for-byte untouched after load, including its comment. The round trip is equal.\n- `backend_configuration_round_trips_without_materializing_defaults`: a rich Bedrock and Ollama config with nested options round-trips. The serialized text has no `endpoint`, no `http://localhost` and no `concurrency`.\n- `authoring_concurrency_defaults_overrides_and_invalid_values`: an omitted `concurrency` stays omitted after serialization, while an explicit value is preserved and round-trips. Invalid values are rejected.\n- `ollama_authentication_resolves_separately_and_rejects_missing_values`: the environment secret is resolved at runtime but `private-value` never appears in the serialized output. The env var name does.\n- `explicit_credentials_round_trip_but_diagnostics_and_debug_redact_them`: explicit credentials round-trip, Debug output is redacted, and invalid combinations are rejected.\n- `user_and_workspace_authoring_tables_round_trip_every_map`: round trips for projects, an explicitly empty `configs`, and workspace configs and options.\n- `workspace_discovery_uses_nearest_file_without_merging_or_rewriting_it`: the workspace file is unchanged after load, and omitted `configs` stays omitted in the output.\n- `workspace_defaults_and_configs_combine_with_existing_user_selections`: resolution does not change the loaded configs.\n\n**Residual gaps.**\n- There is no test that drives a full authoring run and checks the config file afterwards.\n- No test targets Bedrock profile or region omission at the serialization level specifically.\n\nBoth are covered structurally: no writer exists, and the resolved types are not serializable. Neither warrants a failing judgment.",
  "references": "8568e11156882e5f9fa3ff681455163831d68d1a4f6816b022e994dbd51f1dea",
